AUDIOPLAY(1)                    User Commands                     AUDIOPLAY(1)

NAME
       audioplay - play sound files through the audio service

SYNOPSIS
       audioplay [options] [FILE...]
       audioplay -l FILE...
       audioplay --raw -r RATE -c CHANNELS -f FORMAT [FILE|-]

DESCRIPTION
       audioplay plays each FILE in turn through the audio service, audiod(1),
       which mixes it with the sound of every other program. With no FILE,  or
       with -, it plays standard input.

       The uncompressed and simply coded  formats  are  decoded  by  audioplay
       itself:

       WAV -- RIFF, big-endian RIFX and RF64/BW64 for files over 4 GB; integer
       PCM of 8, 16, 24 and 32 bits (and 12 or 20 bits in  wider  containers),
       IEEE float of 32 and 64 bits, G.711 mu-law and  A-law,  IMA  ADPCM  and
       Microsoft ADPCM; all of these also inside WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE, whose
       speaker mask -l shows.

       Sun/NeXT AU (.au, .snd) -- linear 8, 16, 24 and 32 bit, float,  double,
       mu-law and A-law.

       AIFF and AIFF-C -- big-endian PCM of 8  to  32  bits,  and  the  AIFF-C
       codings NONE, twos, sowt (little-endian), raw (unsigned),  in24,  in32,
       fl32, fl64, ulaw and alaw.

       CAF (Core Audio) -- linear PCM in either byte order, float, mu-law  and
       A-law.

       Raw PCM without a header, described with -r, -c and -f.

       Files may have 1 to 8 channels at any rate  from  4000  to  384000  Hz.
       audioplay only changes how the samples  are  represented  (byte  order,
       sign, G.711 and ADPCM expansion);  the  service  converts  the  format,
       resamples to the rate of the sound card and maps  the  channels,  so  a
       24-bit or 96 kHz file reaches the mixer with all its resolution.

       The type of a file is taken from its contents, not its name. A WAV file
       whose header gives no length, as written by programs that record into a
       pipe, plays to its end. A file that claims  more  data  than  it  holds
       plays what is there.

       Compressed formats -- MP3, Ogg Vorbis and Opus, FLAC, AAC and M4A, WMA,
       Matroska and WebM, and WAV or CAF files that carry one of those --  are
       not  decoded  here.  When  GStreamer's  gst-play-1.0(1)  is  installed,
       audioplay runs it on such a file (it replaces  itself  with  it  for  a
       single file); otherwise it says what the file is and how to convert it.

       Successive files of the same format play through one stream  without  a
       gap, as an album should.

       Ctrl-C stops playback: what is still buffered is dropped, the stream is
       closed and audioplay exits with status 130. A second  Ctrl-C  exits  at
       once.

OPTIONS
       -v, --volume N
              The stream's  volume,  0..100  (100,  the  default,  leaves  the
              samples as they are). It applies on top of the master volume and
              can be changed while playing with audioctl stream-volume.

       -R, --role ROLE
              What the sound is for: music (the default), video, game,  event,
              phone, web or other. audioctl streams shows it.

       -L N, --loop[=N]
              Play each file N times in all; --loop without  N,  and  N  =  0,
              repeat until interrupted. Standard input and pipes cannot loop.

       -s, --start TIME
              Begin TIME into each file. TIME is seconds (90,  12.5),  minutes
              and seconds  (1:30,  1:30.25)  or  hours,  minutes  and  seconds
              (1:02:03). On a pipe the samples before the start are  read  and
              dropped.

       -t, --duration TIME
              Play at most TIME of each file (with --loop: of each pass).

       -p, --progress
              Show the position, the length and a bar on standard error  while
              playing. The position is what has been heard, not what has  been
              sent. Ignored when standard error is not a terminal.

       -l, --list
              Print what each file is -- container, coding, rate, channels and
              speaker layout, length, data size and bit rate, what is sent  to
              the service, and the title, artist, album, date, genre,  comment
              and copyright found in it -- and play nothing.

       -n, --name NAME
              The stream's name as audioctl streams shows it. By default it is
              "artist - title" from the file's tags, or the title, or the file
              name.

       -b, --buffer MS
              How much sound the service should hold for the stream,  1..10000
              milliseconds. Larger survives a busy system  better  and  reacts
              later to pause and volume; the default is the  service's  (about
              100 ms).

       -q, --quiet
              No messages except errors.

       --no-gst
              Never hand a file to gst-play-1.0.

       -h, --help
              A summary of the options.

       -V, --version
              The version.

RAW PCM
       A file with no header audioplay recognises is played as  raw  PCM  when
       any of the following is given; --raw forces it even when the file has a
       header.

       --raw  Treat every input as headerless PCM.

       -r, --rate HZ
              Sample rate, 4000..384000; 44.1k and 48k are  accepted.  Default
              48000.

       -c, --channels N
              Interleaved channels, 1..8. Default 2.

       -f, --format FMT
              Sample format: u8, s8, s16le (s16), s16be, u16le,  u16be,  s24le
              (packed 3 bytes), s24be, s24_32le (24 bits in the  low  bits  of
              32), s32le, s32be, f32le (float), f32be, f64le (double),  f64be,
              ulaw, alaw.  The  service's  names  (S16_LE,  F32_LE,  ...)  are
              accepted too. Default s16le.

EXAMPLES
       audioplay /usr/share/sounds/likeos/startup.wav
       audioplay -p *.wav                 an album, gapless, with progress
       audioplay -L 0 -v 30 rain.aiff     loop quietly until Ctrl-C
       audioplay -s 1:30 -t 20 song.wav   20 seconds from 1:30
       audioplay -l recording.wav         what is it?
       cat voice.raw | audioplay -r 16k -c 1 -f s16le
       audioplay song.mp3                 played with gst-play-1.0

EXIT STATUS
       0      Everything played.

       1      A file could not be opened or  decoded,  or  the  audio  service
              could not be reached or failed during  playback.  Further  files
              are still played.

       2      A bad option.

       130    Interrupted by Ctrl-C (128 plus  the  signal  number  for  other
              signals).

FILES
       /var/run/audiod/socket
              The audio service's socket.

       /usr/share/sounds/likeos
              The system sounds; see eventsound(1).

SEE ALSO
       audioctl(1), audiotest(1), eventsound(1), gst-play-1.0(1), audiod(1)

